/tmp/arxiv_1901_09349_src/appendix_345.tex:47:\section{\proofof{\Cref{claim: short paths in expanders}}}\label{subsec: proof of short paths in expanders}
/tmp/arxiv_1901_09349_src/appendix_345.tex:49:Consider the following sequence of vertex subsets. Let $S_0=Z$, and for all $i>0$, let $S_i$ contain all vertices of $S_{i-1}$, and all neighbors of vertices in $S_{i-1}$. Notice that, if $|S_{i-1}|\leq |V(T)|/2$, then, since $T$ is an $\alpha'$-expander, there are at least $\alpha' |S_{i-1}|$ edges leaving the set $S_{i-1}$, and, since the maximum vertex degree in $T$ is at most $d$, there are at least $\frac{\alpha'|S_{i-1}|}{d}$ vertices that do not belong to $S_{i-1}$, but are neighbors of vertices in $S_{i-1}$. Therefore, $|S_i|\geq |S_{i-1}|\left (1+\frac{\alpha'}{d}\right )$.
